## Payments

Card payments through {{ prov.title }}'s hosted checkout ({{ prov.checkout_name }}), in `app/payments/`. **Card numbers never
touch this code**: the payer enters them on the provider's page.

| Route | What it does |
|---|---|
| `POST /payments` | Start a payment for one {{ pay.payable.entity }} (send `Idempotency-Key`); returns the `checkout_url` to send the payer to. The amount is read from the {{ pay.payable.entity }} on the server |
| `GET /payments`, `GET /payments/{id}` | Your payments (or everyone's, with the `read_all` roles) |
| `POST /payments/{id}/cancel` | Abandon a checkout that has not been paid |
| `POST /payments/{id}/refunds` | Refund all or part of a payment (send `Idempotency-Key`){% if pay.refund_approval_threshold_minor is not none %}; above {{ pay.refund_approval_threshold_minor }} minor units a second person must approve it{% endif %} |
| `POST /payments/refunds/{id}/approve`, `.../reject` | The second person's decision |
| `GET /payments/{id}/refunds`, `GET /payments/{id}/disputes` | Refunds and disputes of a payment |
| `GET /payments/ops/status`, `/ops/dead-jobs`, `/ops/audit/{id}/verify` | Queue health, failed jobs, audit-trail check |
| `POST /payments/webhooks/{{ pay.provider }}` | The provider calls this; not for you. Signature-checked |

Who may do what is set by the roles you chose when configuring payments; an empty list means
nobody. `docker compose up --build` starts the API and `payments-worker`, which must be running for
refunds and for webhook processing in `queued` mode. Set {% for name in prov.key_envs %}`{{ name }}`, {% endfor %}and
`{{ prov.webhook_secret_env }}` in `.env`, then follow **`deploy/DEPLOYMENT.md`** for registering the
webhook, sizing, running workers on other machines, going live and operating it.
`make test` runs the payment tests against a stand-in for the provider's API.
{% endif %}
